-- title:
--   Density of Paley–Wiener slab profiles on a determinant slab
-- statement:
--   Let $F$ be a number field, let $\alpha$ be the character of the idele group $(\mathbb{A}_F)^\times$ obtained from the distributive Haar character of $\mathbb{A}_F$ by viewing its nonnegative real values as real units, and assume $\alpha(x)>0$ for all $x$. Fix reals $0<d_1<d_2$, a set $\Phi\subseteq \mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb{A}_F)$ (entering only as the domain datum of the carrier pins `productionPinsOf F Φ …`, whose central subgroup $Z$ is all of $(\mathbb{A}_F)^\times$), a continuous character $\xi : Z \to \mathbb{C}^\times$ with $|\xi(z)|=1$, and a function $\varphi : \mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb{A}_F)\to\mathbb{C}$ which is a slab profile for $\xi$: measurable, invariant under left multiplication by unipotent matrices $\begin{pmatrix}1&x\\0&1\end{pmatrix}$, $x\in\mathbb{A}_F$, and by global matrices with vanishing lower-left entry, transforming by $\xi$ under the central ideles, bounded on every determinant slab $\{\,\|\det g\|\in[d_1',d_2']\,\}$ with $d_1'>0$, and supported in some band $a\le \mathrm{adelicHeight}_F(g)\le b$ with $a>0$. Then there exist $a,b$ with $0<a$ such that $\varphi(g)\neq0$ implies $\mathrm{adelicHeight}_F(g)\in[a,b]$, and for every $\varepsilon>0$ there are a finite index type $\iota$, families $\mu,\nu:\iota\to\mathrm{Hom}((\mathbb{A}_F)^\times,\mathbb{C}^\times)$, a map $r:\iota\to\iota$, a family $\psi_f:\iota\to\mathbb{C}\to\mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb{A}_F)\to\mathbb{C}$ and a function $\psi$ such that: each $\mu_e,\nu_e$ is unitary (all values of absolute value $1$), trivial on the principal ideles $F^\times$, and continuous; $\mu_e(z)\nu_e(z)=\xi(z)$ for all $z\in Z$; $\mu_{r e}=\nu_e$ and $\nu_{r e}=\mu_e$; distinct $e\neq e'$ are separated by some norm-one idele, where $\mu_e$ or $\nu_e$ differs from $\mu_{e'}$ or $\nu_{e'}$; for every $s$, $\psi_f(e,s)$ satisfies the induced-section identity $\psi_f(e,s)(bg)=\chi_1(b_{11})\chi_2(b_{22})\psi_f(e,s)(g)$ for $b$ with $b_{21}=0$, where $\chi_1=\mu_e\,\alpha^{\,s+1/2}$ and $\chi_2=\nu_e\,\alpha^{-(s+1/2)}$; $(s,g)\mapsto\psi_f(e,s)(g)$ is continuous, $s\mapsto\psi_f(e,s)(g)$ is entire, each $\psi_f(e,s)$ is arch-$K$-finite (its right translates under the row-isometry subgroup at each infinite place span a finite-dimensional space) and $K_f$-smooth (its stabiliser in the kernel of $\mathrm{glArch}$ is open), and for each $e$ and each infinite place $w$ a single finite-dimensional subspace $W$ contains all the functions $k\mapsto\psi_f(e,s)(gk)$ on the row-isometry subgroup at $w$, uniformly in $s$ and $g$; for each $e$, each $n\in\mathbb{N}$, each $\sigma_0$ and each compact $C$ there is an integrable, bounded-above $m:\mathbb{R}\to\mathbb{R}$ with $(1+|t|)^n\|\psi_f(e,\sigma'+it)(g)\|\le m(t)$ for all $|\sigma'|\le\sigma_0$, $t\in\mathbb{R}$, $g\in C$; $\psi$ is again a slab profile for $\xi$, is given for every $\sigma'\in\mathbb{R}$ by $\psi(g)=\sum_{e}(4\pi)^{-1}\int_{\mathbb{R}}\psi_f(e,\sigma'+it)(g)\,dt$, and satisfies the same height band $[a,b]$; and finally the $L^2$-norm of $q\mapsto \varphi(q^{\mathrm{out}})-\psi(q^{\mathrm{out}})$ on the quotient of $\mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb{A}_F)$ by the orbit relation of the subgroup generated by the rational torus and the adelic unipotent group, for the quotient Haar measure restricted to $\{q : \|\det q^{\mathrm{out}}\|\in[d_1,d_2]\}$, is less than $\varepsilon$.
--
--   This is the density statement underlying the spectral (Parseval) pairing on the unitary axis: slab profiles on the determinant slab are approximated in $L^2$ of $T(F)N(\mathbb{A})\backslash \mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb{A})$ by Paley–Wiener profiles, synthesised from finitely many pairs of unitary idele class characters with product $\xi$ through entire, vertically rapidly decreasing families of induced sections of fixed $K_\infty$-type. It is used by the comparison of truncated integrals of pseudo-Eisenstein series against their conjugates and by the vanishing statement for such integrals for arbitrary slab profiles.
